Section 2: Data Modeling & Immutability RulesCore Entities: Construct normalized relational tables for users, teams, memberships (mapping users to teams with specific roles like Viewer, Member, Lead, Admin), work_items, activity_events, comments, idempotency_keys, and an outbox or jobs table.   Work Item Schema: The work_items table must include fields for title, description, type, priority, status, team assignment, current assignee, creator, due date, timestamps, and crucially, an integer version column for concurrency control.   The Atomic Immutability Rule: History and accountability are paramount. Every state transition, priority change, or ownership transfer must be recorded as an append-only event in the activity_events table. To prevent data mismatch, the agent must ensure that the UPDATE to the work item and the INSERT into the activity log occur strictly within the same database transaction. Updating the item and writing history in separate steps is a critical failure that must be avoided.   Mock Seeding: Do not build user registration flows. Instead, create a robust initialization script that seeds the database with 3-4 teams, around 10 users with distinct roles, and several thousand sample work items to validate and test server-side pagination performance.